**SEO commonly takes 3–6 months to show measurable results. Treat that benchmark as a planning range rather than a deadline. Crawling, indexation, rankings, clicks and enquiries are separate stages and each stage can move at a different speed.**

Ahrefs reported the 3–6 month range in January 2024 from polls with 3,680 respondents on LinkedIn and X. The poll reflects practitioner expectations. It is not a controlled experiment, a UK-specific study or a promise that every campaign will progress on the same schedule.

## What counts as an SEO result?

An SEO result is a measured change tied to a defined goal. "Google found the page" is useful progress but it is not the same result as a qualified enquiry.

Track these stages separately:

- **Discovery and crawling:** Googlebot can find and request the URL.
- **Indexation:** Google chooses to include the URL in its index.
- **Search visibility:** the URL gains impressions for relevant queries.
- **Rankings:** average position improves across a stable query set.
- **Qualified clicks:** people reach the page from relevant searches.
- **Enquiries or sales:** visitors complete the commercial action you value.

Google says a requested recrawl can take from a few days to a few weeks. Google also says a crawl request does not guarantee indexation. This makes a one-week ranking promise unrealistic even when a technical change is correct.

## What should happen during the first six months?

The first six months need dated checkpoints rather than a promise that traffic will rise every week. The milestones below describe what to inspect. They do not guarantee that each signal will appear inside the period.

### Before work starts: record the baseline

Record the priority pages, query groups, conversions and current technical state. Export 8–12 weeks of Search Console data when enough history exists. Note migrations, seasonality and other marketing changes.

### Weeks 1–4: confirm discovery and implementation

Check whether priority URLs are crawlable, indexable and internally linked. Confirm that agreed changes are live through URL Inspection, crawl evidence and page checks.

### Months 2–3: look for relevant search visibility

Check whether target pages gain impressions for the intended query groups. Review query relevance as well as total volume. Growth from irrelevant searches does not show that the page is reaching the right audience.

### Months 3–6: judge direction and traffic quality

Compare equivalent periods and look for a sustained direction in impressions and clicks. Segment brand and non-brand queries where that distinction matters. Review qualified enquiries separately from all organic visits.

The 3–6 month point is a decision window. Keep useful work moving when the leading indicators support it. Change the plan when the intended pages still have no relevant visibility or when traffic does not match the commercial goal.

### At month 6: diagnose the first stalled stage

Do not respond to weak progress by publishing more pages automatically. Find the first broken stage in the path from crawling to enquiries. Then choose a change that addresses that stage.

Ahrefs' 2025 ranking study suggests reviewing pages that remain outside the top 10 after about six months. That is a prompt to update the work and evidence. It is not proof that six months of age causes a page to rank.

## What does the Ahrefs research actually show?

Two Ahrefs sources answer different questions. The 2024 poll reports how long respondents believe SEO takes to show results. The 2025 study observes whether sampled URLs reached Google's top 10 within one year.

The May 2025 study reports two URL cohorts:

- **1.74%:** 1 million random URLs first seen by the Ahrefs crawler in September 2023 reached the top 10 for at least one keyword within a year.
- **6.11%:** a different sample of 2 million random URLs created in October 2023 reached the top 10 within a year after Ahrefs filtered for non-empty English content.

{% chart type="bar" eyebrow="Ahrefs cohort study" title="Share of sampled URLs reaching Google's top 10 within a year" data="Sept 2023 cohort:1.74|Oct 2023 cohort:6.11" suffix="%" source="Ahrefs URL cohort study, May 2025" /%}

The percentages are not interchangeable. The second cohort uses a different date and a narrower content filter. Neither cohort measures UK businesses alone, enquiries or the effect of buying an SEO service.

The study also examined top-10 results for 1.3 million random US keywords and found that 72.9% of those ranking pages were more than three years old. This correlation does not show that waiting makes a page rank. Older pages may also have stronger links, better content and more updates. Do not turn either percentage into a forecast for one website.

{% stat value="72.9%" label="of top-10 US keyword results were pages more than three years old" source="Ahrefs URL cohort study, 1.3 million US keywords, May 2025" /%}

## What changes the SEO timeline?

SEO timing changes with the starting condition and the size of the ranking task. Six factors deserve an explicit check.

- **Technical access:** blocked crawling, conflicting canonicals and rendering problems can prevent evaluation.
- **Existing visibility:** a page already ranking on page two has a different task from a new URL with no impressions.
- **Search intent:** a page aimed at the wrong task may be indexed without becoming competitive.
- **Competition:** strong results with better evidence, links and brand demand raise the work required.
- **Execution:** delayed development, irregular publishing and missing approvals extend the elapsed time even when the strategy is sound.
- **Demand and conversion:** rankings cannot create search volume and clicks cannot compensate for a weak offer or broken form.

## What should you check when SEO progress stalls?

Start with the first stage that lacks evidence. This keeps the response specific.

1. **The page is not crawled:** check internal discovery, robots rules, response codes and sitemap inclusion.
2. **The page is crawled but not indexed:** check canonical signals, duplication, page value and indexation directives.
3. **The page is indexed but has no relevant impressions:** review the target query, search intent, page focus, internal links and competitive gap.
4. **The page has impressions but few clicks:** inspect the actual result page, average position trend, title, snippet and competing search features.
5. **The page gets clicks but no qualified enquiries:** review visitor intent, offer clarity, conversion path and measurement.

Use a [technical SEO audit](/services/technical-seo-audit/) when crawling, indexation, templates or implementation are the likely constraint. Use content and offer review when the technical path works but the page attracts the wrong searches or fails to convert.

Change one major assumption at a time where practical. Record the change date and the result you expect before the next review.

## How long does SEO take to generate enquiries or revenue?

SEO can only generate enquiries after the relevant visibility and click stages work. The time to revenue therefore depends on search demand, ranking progress, click-through rate, conversion rate, sales quality and transaction value.

Track qualified form submissions, calls or purchases separately from all organic sessions. Avoid assigning every conversion to one SEO change because other marketing and sales work can influence the same journey.

## What happens when you stop SEO work?

Stopping work does not trigger a fixed decline schedule. Some pages keep their positions for long periods. Other pages lose visibility when competitors improve, search intent changes, technical faults appear or the information becomes outdated.

Keep monitoring even when active production pauses. Review critical templates after releases and refresh pages when evidence, products or search behaviour changes. Resume investigation when relevant impressions, clicks or enquiries fall across comparable periods.

## How can you track SEO progress?

Track one row per review point and preserve the evidence behind each decision. The downloadable sheet includes columns for crawling, indexation, impressions, clicks, qualified enquiries, changes, owners and the next review date.

[Download the SEO progress tracker (CSV) →](/downloads/seo-progress-tracker.csv)

Review monthly trends after the first implementation checks. Google recommends focusing more on trends in impressions and clicks than on average position alone. Use the same page and query groups at each review so the comparison remains useful.

## Real launch-to-traction data from my own sites {% #launch-data %}

I run 11 local-SEO lead-generation sites that all launched on 3 August 2026. All 11 had Search Console impressions on day one. After 46 days, none had reached 100 clicks, four had no clicks at all, and the best had 6.

| Site | Days to first click | Total clicks (46 days) |
|---|---|---|
| seosheffield.uk | 4 | 6 |
| seotelford.uk | 4 | 2 |
| seomiltonkeynes.uk | 10 | 2 |
| seowigan.uk | 11 | 1 |
| seo-cambridge.uk | 21 | 3 |
| seocoventry.uk | 28 | 1 |
| seoleicester.uk | 43 | 1 |
| seohertfordshire.uk | no click in 46 days | 0 |
| seo-middlesbrough.com | no click in 46 days | 0 |
| seowakefield.uk | no click in 46 days | 0 |
| seoandover.uk | no click in 46 days | 0 |

Source: Search Console, pulled 18 September 2026, window 3 August to 17 September 2026.

- **Every site was indexed and showing impressions on day one.**
- **Only 7 of 11 recorded any click within 46 days.** Median time to first click among those 7 was 11 days. The other 4 were indexed from day one and never got a visitor.
- **None reached 100 clicks.** Totals ranged from 0 to 6, median 1, 16 clicks across all 11 sites.

{% chart type="bar" eyebrow="Launch cohort" title="Days to first click, the 7 of 11 new sites that got one" data="seosheffield.uk:4|seotelford.uk:4|seomiltonkeynes.uk:10|seowigan.uk:11|seo-cambridge.uk:21|seocoventry.uk:28|seoleicester.uk:43" suffix=" days" source="Search Console, own data, pulled 18 September 2026" /%}

Small, single-niche, single-operator sample. One point travels: day-one indexation bought these sites nothing. Clicks are a separate stage with its own clock.

{% pullquote cite="On the 11-site launch cohort" %}
One point travels: day-one indexation bought these sites nothing. Clicks are a separate stage with its own clock.
{% /pullquote %}

## Which sources support this timeline?

The timeline uses current primary documentation and clearly labels what each source measures.

- [Ahrefs practitioner poll: How Long Does SEO Take to Show Results?](https://ahrefs.com/blog/how-long-does-seo-take/) — updated 31 January 2024; 3,680 LinkedIn and X respondents.
- [Ahrefs URL cohort study: How Long Does It Take to Rank in Google?](https://ahrefs.com/blog/how-long-does-it-take-to-rank-in-google-and-how-old-are-top-ranking-pages/) — published 15 May 2025; separate 1 million and 2 million URL cohorts.
- [Google Search Central: Ask Google to Recrawl Your Website](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/ask-google-to-recrawl) — crawling can take days to weeks and a request does not guarantee indexation.
- [Google Search Console: Performance report guidance](https://support.google.com/webmasters/answer/17010961?hl=en) — use trends in impressions and clicks rather than position alone.

